---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const title = 'How to use';
const description =
  'Operator manual covering wizard mode, command-line flags, release signing, memory profiles, and automated builds.';
const repo = 'C:\\path\\to\\android-apk-builder';
const proj = 'C:\\your-android-project';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} wide>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>How to use</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Choose between two interfaces. The <code>build.bat</code> launcher provides
      interactive prompts and quick CLI access, while <code>setup-windows.ps1</code>
      exposes the full PowerShell engine. Both run on Windows 10 and 11 without
      Android Studio.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Wizard mode</h2>
  <p>
    Double-click <code>build.bat</code> in the builder directory. Because that
    directory is not an Android project, the wizard prompts for the project path.
    Enter a folder path or drag and drop a folder into the window. Then choose an
    action:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to build the default Release APK.</li>
    <li>
      Press <kbd>C</kbd> and then <kbd>Enter</kbd> to customize build variants,
      artifact types, clean options, or memory modes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Dragging and dropping a project folder onto <code>build.bat</code> in File
    Explorer builds that project immediately. The terminal stays open afterward
    so you can inspect output paths and signature verification details.
  </p>

  <h2>Command-line invocations</h2>
  <p>Run from within the Android project directory:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Build the current project" code={`cd ${proj}\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Release`} />
  <p>Or pass the path explicitly from any location:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Explicit project directory" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ProjectDir "${proj}" -BuildType Release`} />

  <h3>Build variants</h3>
  <CodeBlock caption="Release, debug, or both" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Debug\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Release\n${repo}\\build.bat -BuildType Both`} />

  <h3>Artifact types</h3>
  <p>
    Generate installable APK files (default), Google Play AAB bundles, or both.
    Google Play requires AAB bundles for store releases, while APKs install
    directly on test devices.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="APK, AAB, or both" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ArtifactType APK\n${repo}\\build.bat -ArtifactType AAB\n${repo}\\build.bat -ArtifactType Both`} />

  <h3>Multi-module apps</h3>
  <p>For projects with multiple app modules, pass <code>-Module</code> to target a specific module:</p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Target one module" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -Module ":app" -BuildType Release`} />

  <h2>Release signing</h2>
  <p>
    When no keystore is provided, the engine creates a dedicated release
    keystore, signs the APK with v1, v2, and v3 schemes, and preserves the key
    for subsequent builds. To sign with an existing keystore, pass your
    credentials:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Custom keystore" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -KeystorePath "C:\\keys\\app.jks"\n-StorePassword "secret123" -KeyAlias "myalias"`} />
  <p>
    To let Gradle handle release signing, pass <code>-SkipSigning</code>. You can
    also specify certificate details using
    <code>-KeyDName "CN=Dev,O=Org,C=US"</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Hardware profiles</h2>
  <dl class="profiles-grid">
    <div class="profile-card">
      <dt><code>-ForceLowMem</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Caps JVM heap memory at 1.5 GB, limits execution to a single worker, and
        disables the Gradle daemon. Recommended for systems with 4 GB to 8 GB of
        RAM to prevent excessive disk paging.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="profile-card">
      <dt><code>-HighMem</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Allocates a 4 GB heap, enables parallel workers, and turns on the build
        cache for multi-core workstations.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="profile-card">
      <dt>Default</dt>
      <dd>
        The engine detects physical RAM through WMI and selects a profile
        automatically. When total system memory is below 8.5 GB, conservative
        limits apply.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <CodeBlock caption="Force a profile" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ForceLowMem\n${repo}\\build.bat -HighMem`} />

  <h2>Automation and CI switches</h2>
  <p>
    For automated pipelines and continuous integration, combine switches such as
    <code>-AcceptLicenses</code> to accept SDK terms non-interactively,
    <code>-CleanFirst</code> to run a clean build,
    <code>-OutputDir "C:\builds"</code> to set a destination folder,
    <code>-Offline</code> to use cached dependencies,
    <code>-DryRun</code> to inspect project requirements without compiling, and
    <code>-VerboseLog</code> for diagnostics.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock caption="Headless example" code={`${repo}\\build.bat -ProjectDir "${proj}"\n-AcceptLicenses -CleanFirst -OutputDir "C:\\builds"`} />

  <h2>What the engine changes on your machine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Environment variables such as <code>JAVA_HOME</code>,
      <code>ANDROID_HOME</code>, and <code>GRADLE_OPTS</code> are scoped
      strictly to the active terminal session. The engine writes nothing to the
      Windows registry or persistent system environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      The engine updates <code>sdk.dir</code> in <code>local.properties</code>
      using forward slashes. It creates a <code>local.properties.bak</code> backup
      first and preserves all other existing lines and comments.
    </li>
    <li>
      The engine copies artifacts from Gradle build folders rather than moving
      them, preserving incremental build caches.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outputs and verification</h2>
  <p>
    Harvested files are saved to the output directory, which defaults to
    <code>dist</code>, and copied to
    <code>android-apk-builder\dist\&lt;project&gt;</code>. For every artifact,
    the engine computes a SHA-256 hash and runs <code>apksigner verify</code> to
    report active signature schemes. The signing manifest file,
    <code>signing-credentials.txt</code>, records passwords, key aliases,
    certificate fingerprints, a Base64-encoded keystore for CI secrets, and a
    GitHub Actions workflow step.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retain generated keystores alongside the project. Installing an updated APK
    signed with a different key triggers
    <code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code> on Android devices.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting issues</h2>
  <p>
    If you encounter build failures or bugs,
    <a href="https://github.com/tanvirr007/android-apk-builder/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
